# Applied by Cloudflare's edge to every static asset response (index.html,
# app.js, styles.css, favicon.svg, theme-init.js) BEFORE they ever reach the
# Worker — see https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# This is necessary, not just belt-and-suspenders: Worker code (the
# `applySecurityHeaders` wrapper in src/index.ts) only runs for requests
# that reach the Worker's fetch handler. With Workers Static Assets
# configured (see [assets] in wrangler.toml), a request for a file that
# exists under public/ is served directly from the edge and NEVER invokes
# the Worker at all — so without this file, none of the Worker's security
# headers would apply to the actual HTML/JS/CSS the browser renders.
#
# The CSP here is scoped to what this specific frontend actually needs, not
# a generic template:
#   - script-src 'self'      — app.js and theme-init.js are same-origin,
#                               loaded via <script src>; there is no inline
#                               script anywhere in index.html (the former
#                               inline theme-flash script was moved to
#                               theme-init.js specifically so this can be
#                               'self' with no 'unsafe-inline').
#   - style-src ... googleapis.com — Google Fonts' stylesheet is the one
#     external resource this page loads. Self-hosting the font files would
#     remove even this, but requires bundling the font binaries as static
#     assets; explicitly scoping to just the required origin (rather than a
#     blanket allowance) is the documented fallback when self-hosting isn't
#     done.
#   - font-src ... gstatic.com — where those Google Fonts' actual font
#     files are served from.
#   - img-src 'self' data: https: — the page's OWN markup only ever loads
#     the local favicon/logo (self), but `data:` and `https:` are required
#     here for a non-obvious reason: a `<iframe srcdoc>` document INHERITS
#     its parent's CSP, and the two policies are INTERSECTED, not
#     overridden. The message viewer's per-message iframe (public/app.js)
#     sets its own stricter `img-src data:` by default and widens it to
#     `img-src data: https:` only after the user clicks "Load images" — but
#     if this top-level policy said `img-src 'self'`, the intersection
#     would clamp it back to 'self' and the "Load images" button would
#     silently do nothing. Allowing https: at the parent level is safe
#     because the page never inserts remote images into its own document
#     (all untrusted email HTML is textContent/srcdoc-only and confined to
#     a `sandbox=""` frame); the tracking-pixel protection is still
#     enforced by the child frame's own policy, which still blocks remote
#     images until the user opts in.
#   - frame-src 'self'        — the message viewer's iframe has no `src`,
#     only `srcdoc`; browsers evaluate frame-src for srcdoc frames against
#     the parent document's own origin, so this must be 'self' (not 'none')
#     for that iframe to render at all.
#   - object-src 'none', base-uri 'self', form-action 'self',
#     frame-ancestors 'none' — standard hardening with no functional cost:
#     this page uses no <object>/<embed>, no <base> tag, and its one form
#     is JS-intercepted (never a real navigation), and must never be framed
#     by another site (clickjacking).

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self'; frame-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'
